## ABSTRACT
Carboxylic acid-based metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are normally passed for the "pre-catalysts" for oxygen evolution reaction (OER) due to the hydroxides constructed in-situ during its alkaline hydrolysis process (AHP) in lye. Whereas, it remains a mystery that they show advantageous activity over prototypical hydroxides when they are directly acted as OER catalysts. Herein, we propose for the first time that the steric hindrance effect of Nafion can induce enhanced catalytic activity of such MOFs. Different from conventional catalysts without AHP, the Nafion with 3D structure weakens the AHP of Co-MOF nanoribbons, thus forming small size and low crystallinity species (cobalt hydroxide) with more active sites. And the existence of Nafion also optimizes its electronic structure, which is confirmed by transmission electron microscopy (TEM), in-situ UV absorption spectra, in-situ Raman spectroscopy and so on. Compared with Co-MOF-K obtained by directly immersing the Co-MOF nanoribbons in 1.0 M KOH, the Co-MOF-NK obtained by AHP of Co-MOF mixed with Nafion shows better catalytic activity. Based on the above inspiration, we realized the low overpotential of \(268\;\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) by preparing CoFe-MOF-NK. This work provides a new understanding of the structural reconstruction of MOFs in the field of electrocatalysis.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks, structural reconstruction, Nafion, stereo hindrance, water oxidation
## 1 Introduction
Vigorously developing clean renewable energy and cost-effective and large-scale energy storage conversion systems has become one of the research hotspots nowadays [1–4]. Oxygen evolution reaction (OER) as one of the key units in the fields of clean energy such as water splitting, fuel cells, and metal-air batteries, has received great attention [5–9]. At present, commercial noble metal-based catalysts \(\mathrm{(RuO_{2}}\) , \(\mathrm{IrO}_{2},\) etc.) with state-of-the-art catalytic activity are hindered in practical application due to their high price and scarcity [10]. Therefore, it is necessary to explore alternative earth-abundant OER electrocatalysts with excellent performance [11–14]. More importantly, a detailed interpretation of OER processes and mechanisms related to a specific class of materials is imperative to design desirable electrocatalysts efficiently and accurately.
Carboxylate linkers have been extensively used for preparing various metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with different dimensions, adjustable aperture, and mixed metals or ligands due to the mild conditions in the coordination reaction of carboxylate and metal ions [15–18]. Recently, carboxylate-based MOFs have emerged as promising candidates in the field of electrocatalysis, and rapidly become a member of the OER catalyst family [19–21]. However, the poor conductivity serves as a shackle, limiting their application in OER, and their excellent crystallinity results in the restriction of active
sites within the framework, which is also detrimental to the OER process [20, 22]. In view of the above shortcomings, the existing strategies include (i) selecting nickel foam as MOFs growth collector to improve the conductivity [23]; (ii) constructing bimetal cooperative MOFs to optimize the electronic structure [24]; (iii) designing low crystallinity MOFs to enrich the active site [20]. Unfortunately, although these MOF catalysts show excellent catalytic activity, the catalytic mechanism is still unclear.
