active phase may also be affected by other factors. Therefore, it is of great significance to investigate the influencing factors in the AHP of carboxylic acid-based MOFs on the structural properties (such as crystallinity, size, etc.) of the active phase, so as to further understand its advantageous superior catalytic activity.
In this work, the causes of the advantageous OER activity exhibited by carboxylic acid-based MOFs are explored for the first time. Different from the traditional stable catalyst, the alkali instability of Co-MOF makes Nafion not only have conventional effects (such as anchoring the catalyst) but also regulate the structure of alkaline hydrolysate during AHP. As shown in Scheme 1, the structure-activity relationship between AHP and the catalytic activity of Co-MOF was identified by immersing the electrode materials in different states into \(1.0\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH}\). Compared with the direct immersion of Co-MOF nanoribbons in \(1.0\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH}\) (Co-MOF-K, K is for KOH), the \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) active species obtained by AHP of Co-MOF modified by Nafion with a three-dimensional (3D) hydrophobic skeleton structure (Co-MOF-NK, N is for Nafion) exhibits a smaller size and weaker crystallinity, thus showing improved OER catalytic activity (the overpotential decreases by \(34\ \mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\ \mathrm{mA\cdot cm^{-2}}\)). By contrast, the conversion process of MOF with larger sizes was also weakened due to the barrier of Nafion, and only a small number of metal nodes can be converted into active sites, leading to a decrease in OER activity.
## 2 Results and discussion
## 2.1 Structural characterization
of Nafion on the sample surface (Figs. 1(d) and 1(g)). The low magnification scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) and TEM images of Co-MOF-NK show that the small-size \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) forms with a disordered array structure due to the presence of the binder (Figs. S3 and S5 in the ESM). To better understand the state of the working electrode under different test conditions, the working electrode after five linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) tests on Co-MOF-NK was characterized by SEM, TEM, and TEM mapping images. Normally, the morphology of Co-MOF-NK has negligible change after LSV test, indicating that it reaches a relatively stable state (Figs. S6 and S7 in the ESM). The above TEM and SEM results indicate that Nafion plays an important role in the structural reconstruction, especially in the regulation of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) size and crystallinity. Therefore, it is possible to expose more active sites and thus exhibiting excellent electrocatalytic activity compared to traditional hydroxides.
## 2.2  Reconstruction mechanism
Extensive studies have confirmed that Nafion (perfluorinated sulfonic-acid ionomer, consisting of hydrophobic perfluorocarbon backbone and hydrophilic sulfonic side-chain) is the most widely used ionomer due to its inherent outstanding electrochemical stability and robust mechanical matrix [32, 33]. As one of the essential components of fuel cells and other devices, the Nafion was commonly used to anchor the catalyst to achieve the desired device performance. The catalyst layer (CL) on the electrode substrate was usually prepared by drying an equably dropped catalyst ink, which consists of catalyst, ionomer solution, and dispersion. When the CL was put into the electrolyte (water), Nafion was hydrated, and the nanophase-separated structure was formed due to the dissimilar nature between hydrophobic and hydrophilic regions (Fig. S8 in the ESM). The hydration process was shown in Fig. S9 in the ESM, under low hydration, the isolated and spherical ionic clusters were formed in the polymer network. With the increase of hydration, clusters grow, percolate, coalesce, and form locally-flat hydrophilic connecting channels. These nanoscale locally-flat hydrophilic regions surrounded by hydrophobic perfluoro-carbon matrix, and swell with hydration in a mesoscopic scale mesh-work [33, 34].
Based on the interesting features of Nafion and related reports, the ultimate possible structural model of the above post-drying CL was shown in Fig. 2(a), the multistage sandwich-like hydrophilic clusters were surrounded by perfluoro-carbon matrix and then a hydrophobic interface was formed [35]. When the Nafion-attached CL has just been impregnated electrolyte (1.0 M KOH), the perfluorocarbon-rich hydrophobic surface gives high transport resistance to \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\) due to weak phase-separation, and thus hinders the AHP of MOF (Fig. 2(a)) (First step). With the increase of the humidity of the CL, the stronger phase-separation occurs, hydrophilic "active" ionic sites are gradually exposed on the outer surface, which gives lower transport resistance to \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\) and slower hydration process. It should be noted that when the electrolyte is KOH solution, the interaction between \(\mathrm{K}^{+}\) and the sulfonic group is stronger than that of \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\), which further inhibits the hydration process (Fig. 2(b)) (Second step) [36]. More importantly, the 3D nanoscale framework assembled from the hydrophobic perfluorocarbon backbone of Nafion can cause secondary inhibition of \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\) transport and also plays a limited role in the formation of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) (Third step) [33]. All in all, the Nafion acts as a surfactant-like agent and the steric hindrance generated by its adhesion to the Co-MOF surface also inhibits the AHP of Co-MOF, so that the \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and the \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\) cannot fully contact, thus forming the active species in situ with smaller size and weaker crystallinity. Ulteriorly, when the Co-MOF powder and Co-MOF-NK were immersed in 1.0 M KOH solution, respectively, the UV absorption spectra of the electrolyte due to the diffusion of biphenyl-4, 4'-dioxoboxylic acid (bpdc) from the MOFs at different time periods are tested to evaluate the speed of ion exchange in AHP (Fig. 3(a) and 3(b)). Co-MOF powder completed the AHP in just about two minutes, which was far greater than the ion exchange time of Co-MOF-NK (Fig. S10 in the ESM). This further proves that the presence of Nafion slows down the AHP of MOFs in Co-MOF-NK, which was consistent with the theoretical analysis in Fig. 2.
Additionally, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis was operated to understand the AHP of Co-MOF. The survey spectra of Co-MOF and Co-MOF-NK show the presence of Co, O, C, and F elements (Fig. S11 in the ESM), which is in agreement with the energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS)
mapping results. The pristine Co-MOF reveals two peaks at 782.4 and \(798.3\ \mathrm{eV}\) were assigned to the surface \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) species coordinated with bpdc linkers, and the other two peaks at 787.0 and \(803.5\ \mathrm{eV}\) were attributed to the satellite peaks [25]. Importantly, the Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{3}\) - peak position of the Co-MOF-NK has a significant negative shift concerning Co-MOF (from about 782.4 to \(780.6\ \mathrm{eV}\), enlarge the area at the right of Fig. 3(c)). This can be due to the disassembly of the Co-MOF, which directly connects the Co atom to the O atom, enlarging the local electron density, thus decreasing the binding energy of Co 2p in the form of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) [25]. Two pairs of newly added characteristic peaks of the Co-MOF-NK at 780.5 and \(795.5\ \mathrm{eV}\) can be attributed to \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\), caused by surface oxidation of cobalt hydroxide with low crystallinity in Co-MOF-NK or formed during the conversion of Co-MOF to \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\). This active component with a high valence state will also benefit OER catalytic activity [37, 38]. After LSV tests, the Co 2p peaks position of Co-MOF-NK has negligible change compared with that of Co-MOF-NK, only the ratio of \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}/\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) increased slightly (from about 1.64 to 1.77), which was mainly caused by CoOOH formed by an electrocatalytic oxidation process [25]. Meanwhile, the high-resolution XPS spectra of O 1s are shown in Fig. 3(d). Three peaks of pristine Co-MOF positioned at 532.3, 533.4, and \(535.7\ \mathrm{eV}\) were ascribed to \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\), physically adsorbed water, and \(-\mathrm{CF}_{2}\mathrm{-O-CF}_{2}\mathrm{-}\), respectively [39]. After soaking in \(1.0\ \mathrm{M}\) KOH solution, the new peak of Co-MOF-NK centered at about \(530.1\ \mathrm{eV}\) was assigned to the \(\mathrm{O}^{2-}\), which was originated from CoOOH [39]. More importantly, compared with Co-MOF, there was also a significant negative shift in the position of the main oxygen peak of Co-MOF-NK (from about 532.3 to \(530.0\ \mathrm{eV}\)), corresponding to the XPS result of Co 2p. A limited change in the O 1s peak of Co-MOF-NK was shown after LSV tests, which was also consistent with the Co 2p result and the electron microscope (SEM and TEM) results.
## 2.3 Catalytic evaluation
To further investigate the OER catalysis performance of samples obtained under different conditions, the electrochemical evaluation of Co-MOF-K and Co-MOF-NK were measured in \(1.0\ \mathrm{M}\) KOH (Fig. 4). In the LSV curves of Co-MOF-NK, the overpotential at a current density of \(10\ \mathrm{mA\cdot cm}^{-2}\) is \(34\ \mathrm{mV}\) lower than that of the Co-MOF-K (from 1.588 to \(1.554\ \mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE), showing a significant advantage over Co-MOF-K in OER activity. At the potential of \(1.6\ \mathrm{V}\) (vs. RHE), the current density of Co-MOF-NK increases by about \(25\ \mathrm{mA\cdot cm}^{-2}\) (from 14.6 to \(39.9\ \mathrm{mA\cdot cm}^{-2}\)) (Fig. 4(a)). The catalytic performance of Co-MOF-NK was also compared with reported cobalt-based catalysts, exhibiting superior OER catalysis performance (Table S1 in the ESM). The similar Tafel slope values of the Co-MOF-K \((77.7\ \mathrm{mV\cdot dec^{-1}})\) and Co-MOF-NK \((77.5\ \mathrm{mV\cdot dec^{-1}})\) indicate a similar dynamic characteristic of them (Fig. 4(b)). Subsequently, the electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) was evaluated by electrochemical methods to further explore the reason for the superior OER activity of Co-MOF-NK. The cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves of samples at various scan rates \((10{-}50\ \mathrm{mV\cdot s^{-1}})\) in a non-Faradaic range were tested to calculate double-layer capacitance \(\left(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)\), which can indicate the level of the ECSA (Fig. S12 in the ESM) [40]. As shown in Fig. 4(c), Co-MOF-NK exhibits a higher \(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\) \((69.5\ \mathrm{mF\cdot cm}^{-2})\) than Co-MOF-K \((36.4\ \mathrm{mF\cdot cm}^{-2})\), indicating more exposed active sites in Co-MOF-NK. The intrinsic activity of the catalyst is also a key factor for catalytic activity [41, 42]. Fig. 4(d) shows the LSV curves normalized by ECSA, and the weak advantage of Co-MOF-NK indicates that it has better intrinsic activity. In addition, the CV curves measurements of samples at the potential range from \(-0.1\) to \(0.5\ \mathrm{V}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\)) were used to evaluate its electrochemical behavior (Fig. 4(e)). The CV curve of Co-MOF-NK has two pairs of redox peaks, corresponding to species conversion between \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) at
low voltage and species conversion between \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{4+}\) at high voltage [43, 44]. The two peaks obey the following equations
\[
\begin{array}{r}\mathrm{Co(OH)_2+OH^-}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{CoOOH+H_2O+e^-}\\\mathrm{CoOOH+OH^-}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{CoO_2+H_2O+e^-}\end{array}
\]
The presence of \(\mathrm{Co}^{4+}\) may also be due to the further oxidation of \(\mathrm{CoOOH}\) to \(\mathrm{CoO}_{1+x}\mathrm{OH}_{1-x}\) [38]. In short, this \(\mathrm{Co}^{4+}\) species was considered a highly active species in OER catalytic processes [45, 46]. In addition, Co-MOF-NK shows stronger redox peaks than Co-MOF-K, indicating that it may adsorb a higher number of hydroxide ions, thus possessing more active sites [47]. From electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) plots of samples in Fig. 4(f), the ohmic resistance has negligible change, while the Co-MOF-NK shows a smaller semicircle diameter than Co-MOF-K, revealing the improved catalytic activity due to the reduced charge transfer resistance and faster reaction kinetics [48]. The loading capacity of active substances is
also an important index to evaluate the catalytic activity. To better compare the catalytic activities of the Co-MOF-K and Co-MOF-NK, it was assumed that there was no cobalt source loss in the Co-MOF electrode during AHP, and the normalized LSV curve of Co mole shows that the Co-MOF-NK has a more significant catalytic advantage (Fig. S13 in the ESM). In addition, the working electrodes with a general dosage range were also prepared, and the LSV curves show that the Nafion dosage had a negligible effect on the catalytic performance (Fig. S14 in the ESM).
The operando potential dependent Raman spectroscopy was used to study the real-time structural reconstruction and electrocatalytic mechanism of Co-MOF. As shown in Fig. 5(a), the characteristic bands of Co-MOF disappear after the working electrode is immersed in KOH without applying voltage, and the new characteristic band at approximately \(502\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) attributed to \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\), which further confirms the AHP of Co-MOF. At \(0\;\mathrm{V}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\)), the electrode material keeps a relatively
stable state. When the voltage is \(0.1\;\mathrm{V}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\)), a pair of new bands appear at 498 and \(572\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which is derived from CoOOH formed by partial electrooxidation on the surface of \(\mathrm{Co(OH)}_{2}\) \((\mathrm{Co}^{2+} \rightarrow \mathrm{Co}^{3+})\), which is consistent with Fig. 4(e) [39, 49]. After increasing the voltage continuously, the two characteristic bands, especially the band at \(572\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), increased significantly and did not disappear until the voltage reached \(0.5\;\mathrm{V}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\)). At the same time, when the applied voltage was stopped, the two characteristic peaks of CoOOH did not change significantly, which indicated that part of CoOOH formed in the electrochemical process changed into \(\mathrm{CoO}_{2}\) \((\mathrm{Co}^{3+} \rightarrow \mathrm{Co}^{4+})\) species and released oxygen, while the other formed a relatively stable shell. To sum up, when MOF is directly used as an OER catalyst, two irreversible structural reconstructions will occur. The first is the AHP before the electrochemical reaction to form \(\mathrm{Co(OH)}_{2}\) in situ, which is a complete reconstruction. The second one is a layer of thin CoOOH film constructed by electrocatalytic oxidation, which is partially reconstructed [50]. These are consistent with those of XRD, TEM, and electrochemical results.
The above electrochemical test results show that when the Co-MOF was directly acted as OER catalysts, the \(\mathrm{Co(OH)}_{2}\) obtained from Co-MOF in the presence of Nafion show better catalytic activity. Nafion reduced the contact efficiency between the hydroxide ions and Co-MOF in the electrolyte, and slows down the AHP of Co-MOF, and thus obtaining \(\mathrm{Co(OH)}_{2}\) with more active sites. In addition, the adsorption capacity of \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\)is increased and the active ion centers with high valence states are formed. A counter example is given to further study the structural effect of Nafion on carboxylic acid-based Co-MOFs. Specifically, Co-MOF nanoribbons were replaced by Co-MOF(bulk) with block structure (Figs. S15 and S16 in the ESM). Contrary to the above test results, the catalytic activity of Co-MOF(bulk)-K is better than that of Co-MOF(bulk)-NK (Fig. S17 in the ESM). This may be due to the inhibition of its AHP at a certain depth by Nafion, resulting in the conversion of structures only on the surface of the block to the active phase \(\mathrm{Co(OH)}_{2}\), this is also confirmed by the original characteristic peak of Co-MOF(bulk) in the PXRD pattern of Co-MOF(bulk)-NK (Fig. S18 in the ESM). On the contrary, in the absence of Nafion, block MOF can still achieve rapid conversion to \(\mathrm{Co(OH)}_{2}\), leading to the exposure of more active sites in the Co-MOF(bulk)-K than Co-MOF(bulk)-NK. The evaluation of the ECSA (Fig. S22 in the ESM) also confirms the above possibility. In a word, when carboxylic acid-based MOFs are directly used as OER catalysts, Nafion acts as a double-edged sword. It has a positive/negative effect on the precursors with a large/low specific surface area. Therefore, for block MOFs, the design of materials can be inclined to Co-MOF(bulk)-K. Conversely, for small or two-dimensional MOFs, the design of materials can be inclined to Co-MOF-NK.
Different electrochemical methods were used to evaluate the cyclic stability of Co-MOF-NK. Figure S19(a) in the ESM shows a multi-step chronopotentiometric curve for Co-MOF-NK with various current densities from 10 to \(60\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) without IR correction. The current density does not decline significantly after 1,000 cycles of CV (Fig. S19(b) in the ESM). In addition, the continuous electrolysis at a constant applied potential was monitored over \(40\;\mathrm{h}\) (Fig. S19(c) in the ESM). SEM image of Co-MOF-NK after 1,000 cycles was shown in Fig. S20(a) (in the ESM), the hexagonal nanosheets structure with sizes of \(150{-}250\;\mathrm{nm}\) was well maintained. TEM, HRTEM, and EDS mapping further demonstrate its structural stability and uniform distribution of elements (Figs. S20(b)–S20(d) in the ESM). The high-resolution XPS spectra of Co 2p and O 1s were also
analyzed in Figs. 5(b) and 5(c). The value of \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}/\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) in Co 2p increased slightly (from about 1.64 to 1.83), which was mainly caused by further oxidation of the catalyst surface during the electrocatalytic oxidation process. In order to further meet the needs of industrialization, we analyzed the above phenomena and prepared CoFe-MOF-NK with a lamellar structure (Fig. S21 in the ESM). The as-prepared CoFe-MOF-NK delivers a minimum overpotential of \(268\;\mathrm{mV}\) to reach the current density of \(10\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) (Fig. S22 in the ESM). By comparing the normalized current density of and ECSA and \(\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{m}}\), the CoFe-MOF-NK has larger specific surface area and stronger intrinsic catalytic activity than Co-MOF-NK and Fe-MOF-NK, thus showing excellent catalytic activity (Fig. S22 in the ESM). In addition, in the XPS spectra of CoFe-MOF-NK and Fe-MOF-NK (Fig. S23 in the ESM), the Co \(2\mathrm{p}_{2/3}\) peak position of CoFe-MOF-NK shifts to the high binding energy, indicating the strong interaction between Co and Fe, which optimizes the electronic structure and enhances the intrinsic activity.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, the mystery for the beneficial OER activity provided by carboxylic acid-based MOFs was deciphered. We for the first time demonstrate that Nafion can play a regulatory role in enhancing OER performance when structurally unstable carboxylic acid-based Co-MOF (non-alkali resistant) was directly used as OER electrocatalysts in alkaline environments. In this system, the steric hindrance generated by the three-dimensional matrix and hydrophobic perfluorocarbon backbone of Nafion will hinder the AHP of Co-MOF, thus forming \(\mathrm{Co(OH)}_{2}\) with a smaller size, lower crystallinity, more active sites, and better catalytic activity (The overpotential decreases by \(34\;\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\)). This stereo hindrance effect may be beneficial for MOFs with high surface area, and detrimental for MOFs with a low surface area. This finding has important implications for the design of electrochemical applications of MOFs and performance optimization of MOFs as OER catalysts and will also provide insights into the preparation of hydroxides derived from MOFs. Simultaneously, this work also provides a new idea for the research of intermediates of MOF directly used in the OER field.
## 4 Experimental
## 4.1 Materials synthesis
## 4.1.1 Preparation of Co-MOF
Co-MOF was synthesized by the formerly reported work with minor revision [29]. In detail, \(0.4\;\mathrm{mmol}\;\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(0.4\;\mathrm{mmol}\) biphenyl-4, \(4^{\prime}\)-dicarboxylic acid (bpdc) were dispersed into a \(30\;\mathrm{mL}\) mixed solution containing \(26\;\mathrm{mL}\) DMF, \(2\;\mathrm{mL}\) water, and \(2\;\mathrm{mL}\) ethanol. The dispersion was stirred for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) before it was added to the \(50\;\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon autoclave (Anhui Kemi Machinery Technology Co., Ltd.) and then heated at \(120\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(8\;\mathrm{h}\). The dark pink precipitate was washed several times with deionized (DI) water and ethanol and dried at \(60\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) overnight. For the preparation of Fe-MOF and CoFe-MOF, the method is the same as that of Co-MOF, except that \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) is replaced by \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{:}\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) with a molar ratio of 3:1.
## 4.1.2 Preparation of Co-MOF-K and Co-MOF-NK
For Co-MOF-K, the pre-synthesized Co-MOF was soaked in \(10\;\mathrm{M}\) KOH for \(10\;\mathrm{min}\) at room temperature and then washed
several times with DI water and dried at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) overnight. For Co-MOF-NK, the pre-prepared Co-MOF based working electrode was soaked in \(1.0\,\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH for \(10\,\mathrm{\min}\) at room temperature and let dry naturally. The CoFe-MOF-K and CoFe-MOF-NK were prepared in the same way as Co-MOF-K and Co-MOF-NK, the Fe-MOF-NK was prepared in the same way as Co-MOF-NK.
## 4.1.3 Preparation of Co-MOF(bulk)
The Co-MOF(bulk) was synthesized by the formerly reported work [51]. Briefly, \(165\,\mathrm{~mg~CoCl_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}\) and \(186\,\mathrm{~mg~}\,1,4\cdot\) dicarboxybenzene acid (BDC) were dispersed into a \(54~\mathrm{mL}\) mixed solution containing \(48~\mathrm{mL}\) DMF, \(3~\mathrm{mL}\) water, and \(3~\mathrm{mL}\) ethanol. The dispersion was stirred for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) before it was added to the \(100\;\mathrm{mL}\) fenton autoclave and then heated at \(140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) . The dark pink precipitate was washed several times with DI water and ethanol and dried at \(60\,^{\circ}\!C\) overnight.
4.1.4 Preparation of Co-MOF(bulk)-K and Co-MOF(bulk)-NK
The process of prepared Co-MOF(bulk)-K, Co-MOF(bulk)-NK is the same as that of Co-MOF-K, Co-MOF-NK, except that Co-MOF was replaced by Co-MOF(bulk).
## 4.2 Ex-situ characterizations
The phase analysis of the samples was determined by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD, Rigaku MiniFlex600). The morphologies and sizes of the samples were confirmed by scanning electronic microscope (SEM, JSM-7800F) and transmission electron microscope (TEM, JEM-2800) equipped with EDS mapping. HR-TEM and STEM images were also obtained by JEM-2800 operating at an accelerating voltage of \(200~\mathrm{kV}.\) The surface composition and valence state of the samples were proved by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo Scientific ESCALAB 250Xi). In the PXRD patterns of Co-MOF-NK and Co-MOF (bulk)-NK, the sample was obtained by dripping the Co-MOF ink onto a smooth glass plate and soaking it in \(1.0~\mathrm{M}\) KOH solution for ten minutes before scraping the powder down.
## 4.3 In-situ characterizations
The absorbance spectra were collected on a UV-vis spectrophotometer (UV-2550). The absorption spectra were obtained in real time by soaking a certain amount of Co-MOF and Co-MOF-NK in \(1.0~\mathrm{M}\) KOH. The Raman measurement was performed by SR-500I-A with a \(785~\mathrm{nm}\) laser excitation. The Co-MOF-NK electrode immersed in KOH was used directly for in situ Raman spectroelectrochemical study, in which the \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) (1.0 M KCl) and Pt plate were used as the reference electrode and counter electrode, respectively. The In-situ Raman spectra of the working electrode were recorded during a current–time- \((i{-}i)\) curve at selected potentials.
## 4.4 Electrochemical measurements
All electrochemical tests were carried out at room temperature by Chenhua workstation (CHI-760E) with a typical three-electrode system. The \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) (1.0 M KCl) and Pt plate were used as the reference electrode and counter electrode, respectively. To prepare the working electrode, \(3.0~\mathrm{mg}\) of samples (Co-MOF or Co-MOF-K) powder and \(10~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) Nafion solution \((5.0\ \mathrm{wt.}\%)\) were dispersed in a mixed solution \((290~\upmu\mathrm{L},\) \(\mathrm{v_{water}/v_{ethanol}}=3{:}2)\) with a \(30\,\mathrm{\min}\) sonication. Then, the obtained catalyst ink was coated onto a carbon paper (CP), the loading quantity of catalyst was ordered to be \(1.0~\mathrm{mg\cdotcm^{-2}}\) . To evaluate the OER activity of the samples, linear sweep voltammetry (LSV), cyclic voltammetry (CV), and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) tests were carried out in the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) saturation of \(1.0\;\mathrm{M}\) KOH \((\mathrm{pH}=13.96)\) . All the LSV curves were undertaken IR-corrected with a level of \(95\%\) . And the potentials in this work were denoted to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) by the Nernst equation: \(E_{\mathrm{RHE}}\,=\,E_{\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}\,+\,0.059\,\mathrm{\pH}\,+\,0.223\,\mathrm{\V}.\) The electrochemically active surface areas (ECSA) and the normalized current density were calculated by the formula,
(EIS) tests were carried out in the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) saturation of \(1.0\;\mathrm{M}\) KOH \((\mathrm{pH}=13.96)\) . All the LSV curves were undertaken IR-corrected with a level of \(95\%\) . And the potentials in this work were denoted to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) by the Nernst equation: \(E_{\mathrm{RHE}}\,=\,E_{\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}\,+\,0.059\,\mathrm{\pH}\,+\,0.223\,\mathrm{\V}.\) The electrochemically active surface areas (ECSA) and the normalized current density were calculated by the formula,
\[
\mathrm{ECSA}=\frac{C_{\mathrm{dl-samples}}}{C_{\mathrm{dl-carbopaps}}}
\]
\[
j_{\mathrm{ECSA-Normalized}}=\frac{j}{\mathrm{ECSA}}
\]
Where \(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\) is double-layer capacitance, which was calculated by CV test under different sweep scan rates, and \(j\) is the current density.
